{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01204 - MONDO_18435",
  "verbalization": "Mitoxantrone, a蒽醌-based anthracycline antibiotic, is implicated in the treatment of Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) through its ability to inhibit microtubules, a mechanism that is well-documented in its use for certain leukemias. The evidence suggests that Mitoxantrone may be repurposed for AML with the specific chromosomal aberration Inv3(p21;q26.2) or T(3;3)(p21;q26.2), as it is indicated in both inherited and therapy-related AML cases. The shared indication with Idarubicin, a related anthracycline, further supports a potential mechanism of action involving microtubule inhibition, which is a common pathway in chemotherapy for leukemias. The high scores from the three paths indicate a strong, consistent relationship between Mitoxantrone and the target disease, suggesting that the drug may exert its effects through a mechanism that is biologically relevant to the pathogenesis of AML with the specified chromosomal abnormalities. The evidence is supported by the recurring use of Idarubicin as an indication for the disease, reinforcing the possibility of a shared therapeutic pathway.",
